<div class="slide slide--flip">
  <div class="slide__text">
    <h2 class="slide__heading">Our theory</h2>
    <p>We came to participate, not to consume. It would be easier to buy a ticket, watch somebody else's show, and go home. Showing up for us means adding something to the event rather than drawing from it, and what we have to add is our collective skill and a stupid amount of labor. That is the gift, and giving it is the entire point. Nobody here is paid, nobody sells anything, and everything we build is free to whoever wanders in.</p>
    <p>Dancing is not enough. Anyone can point a speaker at a field. What people actually want is to be taken somewhere, to step through a threshold and find that the everyday world does not reach past it. We are not building a stage. We are building a place that swallows you for a while and hands you back changed.</p>
    <p>The goal is disbelief. The reaction we are chasing is somebody stopping at the edge of the tent and asking how on earth this got here. If nobody wonders how we did it, we did not do enough.</p>
    <p>We build it ourselves. Sound, light, structure, environment. None of it is farmed out to vendors who have never seen the vision. We are DJs, artists, sound engineers, lighting designers, and stubborn DIYers, which means everything you see has been imagined by one jelly or another.</p>
    <p>Everything has to come apart. If a piece cannot be broken down, packed flat, and stored between festivals, we do not build it. Modularity is the constraint that lets a volunteer crew put up something this ambitious in a single day.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="slide__visual">
    <img src="/img/build-flooring.webp" alt="Crew on hands and knees under the stretch tent, snapping interlocking floor tiles together">
  </div>
</div>

<div class="slide">
  <div class="slide__text">
    <h2 class="slide__heading">What sets us apart</h2>
    <p>The seams line up, the cables are covered, the sound is tuned, and the thing you walk into feels finished rather than improvised. Most camps this size rent a stage or hire a production company. We are the production company, and nobody gets paid. The whole thing is a gift, which is exactly why we refuse to hand anyone a half built one.</p>
    <p>The other half of it is people. Everyone here is unreasonably good at something, and the whole model is built around finding that thing and getting out of the way. The person who designs the lasers designs the lasers. The person who knows how to talk to festival organizers talks to festival organizers.</p>
    <p>Because we own our infrastructure and document every build, each activation starts from where the last one ended instead of from scratch. We survey ourselves after every event, write down what broke, and fix it before the next one.</p>
    <p>Then we take it all down, pack it out, and leave nothing behind but the photos.</p>
  </div>
